WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.550 --> 00:00:02.092
STEPHANIE WONG: As
a Cloud Developer,

00:00:02.092 --> 00:00:03.610
one realization
you quickly have is

00:00:03.610 --> 00:00:05.530
that despite being
on the cloud, you

00:00:05.530 --> 00:00:08.039
don't want everything
publicly accessible.

00:00:08.039 --> 00:00:09.880
In this episode of
Cloud Networking,

00:00:09.880 --> 00:00:12.160
we cover how to protect
your internal endpoints

00:00:12.160 --> 00:00:13.360
using Cloud NAT.

00:00:13.360 --> 00:00:13.870
Stay tuned.

00:00:20.473 --> 00:00:22.140
Let's say you've got
a cloud application

00:00:22.140 --> 00:00:24.030
with internal
services and you want

00:00:24.030 --> 00:00:26.460
to restrict inbound
communication while still

00:00:26.460 --> 00:00:27.757
allowing outbound.

00:00:27.757 --> 00:00:29.340
One of the traditional
ways to do this

00:00:29.340 --> 00:00:32.220
would be through a VPN service
that secures, authorizes,

00:00:32.220 --> 00:00:36.600
and handles that connection
for you, which is fine but runs

00:00:36.600 --> 00:00:38.370
into a large problem
that you're still

00:00:38.370 --> 00:00:40.890
using public IPs,
which leaves you

00:00:40.890 --> 00:00:43.375
vulnerable to malicious actors.

00:00:43.375 --> 00:00:45.000
Another secure setup
would be something

00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:46.770
called a bastion host--

00:00:46.770 --> 00:00:49.440
basically an external endpoint
which allows your clients

00:00:49.440 --> 00:00:52.050
to SSH from the public
internet and still

00:00:52.050 --> 00:00:55.320
keep your apps from being
public-facing, which,

00:00:55.320 --> 00:00:58.050
again, works, but this
only deals with one

00:00:58.050 --> 00:00:59.160
side of the problem.

00:00:59.160 --> 00:01:01.380
Namely, inbound communication.

00:01:01.380 --> 00:01:03.730
But let's say you have a
multi-tiered application

00:01:03.730 --> 00:01:07.110
setup in the cloud and you have
an update server on premise.

00:01:07.110 --> 00:01:09.870
You want to allow your
instances outbound access

00:01:09.870 --> 00:01:12.450
to the internet for
updates, patching,

00:01:12.450 --> 00:01:16.110
config management, and more
without having an external IP

00:01:16.110 --> 00:01:17.020
address.

00:01:17.020 --> 00:01:18.600
That way, you can
keep your instances

00:01:18.600 --> 00:01:21.750
entirely internet-facing in
a controlled and efficient

00:01:21.750 --> 00:01:22.800
manner.

00:01:22.800 --> 00:01:24.570
For this, it makes
a lot more sense

00:01:24.570 --> 00:01:27.990
to setup a Network
Address Translation, NAT,

00:01:27.990 --> 00:01:31.470
gateway machine, which routes
traffic and lets multiple VMs

00:01:31.470 --> 00:01:34.680
in a subnet reach the internet
using a single public IP

00:01:34.680 --> 00:01:36.100
address.

00:01:36.100 --> 00:01:39.420
Now, normally, this
requires a fair bit of toil.

00:01:39.420 --> 00:01:42.390
Building a high availability
and high bandwidth NAT gateway

00:01:42.390 --> 00:01:45.300
usually requires reserving
static IP addresses,

00:01:45.300 --> 00:01:48.210
creating compute instance
groups as the NAT gateways,

00:01:48.210 --> 00:01:50.640
creating health checks to
monitor their responsiveness,

00:01:50.640 --> 00:01:52.650
and adding default routes
to these instances.

00:01:52.650 --> 00:01:56.280
And with traditional NATs,
you'd have a NAT proxy

00:01:56.280 --> 00:01:58.410
instance between
your cloud instances

00:01:58.410 --> 00:01:59.970
and their destination.

00:01:59.970 --> 00:02:02.430
This means a potential
choke point in the path,

00:02:02.430 --> 00:02:05.850
undermining performance,
throughput, and availability.

00:02:05.850 --> 00:02:09.720
Fortunately, Google Cloud NAT
is a software-defined networking

00:02:09.720 --> 00:02:12.940
SDN solution that
avoids these problems.

00:02:12.940 --> 00:02:15.270
It's a fully managed offering
that lets Google Cloud VM

00:02:15.270 --> 00:02:18.150
instances without
external IP addresses

00:02:18.150 --> 00:02:21.460
and private GKE clusters
connect to the internet.

00:02:21.460 --> 00:02:23.760
It doesn't require custom
routing and simplifies

00:02:23.760 --> 00:02:25.290
and delivers scale,
because it uses

00:02:25.290 --> 00:02:28.330
SDN instead of being an
instance or an appliance

00:02:28.330 --> 00:02:30.090
that you have to manage.

00:02:30.090 --> 00:02:31.890
And better yet,
outside resources

00:02:31.890 --> 00:02:35.220
can't directly access any of the
private instances behind Cloud

00:02:35.220 --> 00:02:39.480
NAT, thereby helping to keep
your VPCs isolated and secure.

00:02:39.480 --> 00:02:40.440
The best part?

00:02:40.440 --> 00:02:42.390
It doesn't use a proxy.

00:02:42.390 --> 00:02:44.310
Instead, each of your
internal instances

00:02:44.310 --> 00:02:47.610
is given a unique set of
NAT IPs and port ranges,

00:02:47.610 --> 00:02:51.630
which are used by Andromeda,
Google's network virtualization

00:02:51.630 --> 00:02:53.550
stack, to perform that.

00:02:53.550 --> 00:02:56.380
This means no choke
points, better scalability,

00:02:56.380 --> 00:02:58.800
performance, and availability.

00:02:58.800 --> 00:03:02.100
Cloud NAT scales seamlessly
with the number of instances

00:03:02.100 --> 00:03:04.350
and the volume of
network traffic.

00:03:04.350 --> 00:03:08.460
And you get as much bandwidth
as instances that do

00:03:08.460 --> 00:03:10.570
have external IP addresses.

00:03:10.570 --> 00:03:13.630
Let's dive into how
to set up Cloud NAT.

00:03:13.630 --> 00:03:17.250
Here I have two VMs set up
in the same VPC and subnet.

00:03:17.250 --> 00:03:19.650
I want web server to be
private, so I've set it up

00:03:19.650 --> 00:03:21.750
with no external IP address.

00:03:21.750 --> 00:03:24.540
In order to access it,
I've set up a second VM

00:03:24.540 --> 00:03:28.140
to act as a bastion host,
which does have an external IP

00:03:28.140 --> 00:03:31.620
and allows for inbound
traffic to my web server.

00:03:31.620 --> 00:03:33.180
I've also set up
firewall rules so

00:03:33.180 --> 00:03:36.180
that I can only use the bastion
host to SSH into the web

00:03:36.180 --> 00:03:37.270
server.

00:03:37.270 --> 00:03:41.320
I'm using the local terminal
to SSH into the bastion host

00:03:41.320 --> 00:03:43.652
to then SSH into the web server.

00:03:43.652 --> 00:03:45.360
You can see here I
don't have the ability

00:03:45.360 --> 00:03:47.520
to access the public
internet, because when

00:03:47.520 --> 00:03:52.270
I try to pull content from
example.com, it hangs.

00:03:52.270 --> 00:03:54.840
Now, let's set up Cloud
NAT to route egress traffic

00:03:54.840 --> 00:03:57.630
from the web server to the
internet using Cloud NAT's

00:03:57.630 --> 00:04:00.000
allocation of IP addresses.

00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:04.810
Head to the Google Cloud NAT
page and click Get Started.

00:04:04.810 --> 00:04:06.280
Enter a gateway name.

00:04:06.280 --> 00:04:10.830
Choose the VPC network
your instances are in.

00:04:10.830 --> 00:04:12.900
Set the region for
the NAT gateway,

00:04:12.900 --> 00:04:15.530
which should be the same as
where your instances are.

00:04:18.260 --> 00:04:23.070
Now create a Cloud Router in
the region and give it a name.

00:04:23.070 --> 00:04:25.950
Leave the rest as
default. Leave the NAT IP

00:04:25.950 --> 00:04:29.610
addresses to automatic, which
is recommended and automatically

00:04:29.610 --> 00:04:32.580
allocates IP addresses
based on usage.

00:04:32.580 --> 00:04:35.880
These IPs are used to translate
the internal addresses

00:04:35.880 --> 00:04:37.530
of instances.

00:04:37.530 --> 00:04:38.685
And now click Create.

00:04:42.280 --> 00:04:44.560
Now, going back to my
web server instance,

00:04:44.560 --> 00:04:48.700
I can now access example.com and
see its content while it still

00:04:48.700 --> 00:04:51.610
has no external IP address.

00:04:51.610 --> 00:04:54.040
It's important to note that
Cloud NAT doesn't set up

00:04:54.040 --> 00:04:54.850
inbound NAT.

00:04:54.850 --> 00:04:57.460
So instances outside
your VPC can't

00:04:57.460 --> 00:04:59.620
initiate their own new
connections to your cloud

00:04:59.620 --> 00:05:01.090
instances with NAT.

00:05:01.090 --> 00:05:03.430
But Cloud NAT is a
great managed service

00:05:03.430 --> 00:05:05.320
for things like fetching
periodic updates

00:05:05.320 --> 00:05:07.702
from an external server
and another network.

00:05:07.702 --> 00:05:09.160
As you grow your
cloud environment,

00:05:09.160 --> 00:05:11.410
centralizing control and
simplifying your network

00:05:11.410 --> 00:05:14.770
topology is going to save
you cycles in the long run.

00:05:14.770 --> 00:05:16.300
Stay tuned for the next episode.

00:05:16.300 --> 00:05:18.550
And remember,
optimizing your network

00:05:18.550 --> 00:05:20.650
means freeing up your bandwidth.

00:05:20.650 --> 00:05:24.000
[MUSIC PLAYING]

